(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-diuretics-k-sparing-cai-quiz"]={"title":"Potassium-Sparing Diuretics, Aldosterone Antagonists & Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors Quiz 1 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does aldosterone do inside a collecting-duct cell?","choices":["Increases the number of sodium channels","Decreases the number of sodium channels","Closes the water channels","Blocks potassium secretion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. After binding its receptor and reaching the nucleus, aldosterone drives protein synthesis that adds sodium channels. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What happens to the diuretic effect of a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor after several days of use?","choices":["It diminishes","It becomes stronger","It stays unchanged","It reverses to sodium retention"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The sodium and potassium loss is short-lived and the effect falls after three to five days of use. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"How are potassium-sparing diuretics most often used?","choices":["Alone for acute pulmonary edema","Alone to prevent mountain sickness","Alone for hypercalcemia with saline","With other diuretics or antihypertensives"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Their weak effect makes them most useful in combination, where they hold on to potassium that the partner drug wastes. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which drug is a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor?","choices":["Amiloride","Acetazolamide","Eplerenone","Torsemide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acetazolamide, dichlorphenamide and methazolamide are the carbonic anhydrase inhibitors. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A climber ascending rapidly wants a drug to prevent mountain sickness. Which diuretic is used?","choices":["Acetazolamide","Furosemide","Spironolactone","Hydrochlorothiazide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mountain sickness is a listed use for carbonic anhydrase inhibitors such as acetazolamide. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A patient taking amiloride is also prescribed an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor. What is the main concern?","choices":["A dangerous fall in potassium","Loss of hearing","A dangerous rise in potassium","Blood clots"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both drugs raise serum potassium, so caution is needed with angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors and angiotensin receptor blockers. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Aldosterone antagonists work best in which circumstance?","choices":["When aldosterone levels are high","When aldosterone levels are low","When serum potassium is low","When urine flow is already high"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These drugs are most effective when circulating aldosterone is high, because they compete for the receptor. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Besides adding sodium channels, which other action does aldosterone have in a collecting-duct cell?","choices":["Lowers sodium-potassium pump activity","Raises sodium-potassium pump activity","Opens the water channels","Stops protein synthesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone also raises the activity of the sodium-potassium pump on the blood side of the cell. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A patient taking triamterene asks about starting a potassium supplement. What is the best advice?","choices":["Take it daily to prevent weakness","Take it only with meals","Double the dose of triamterene","Avoid it unless told otherwise"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Triamterene already holds on to potassium, so a supplement can push potassium dangerously high and needs caution. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which kidney disease with edema is an indication for an aldosterone antagonist?","choices":["Acute tubular necrosis","Kidney stones","Nephrotic syndrome","Polycystic kidney disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nephrotic syndrome is one of the edematous conditions listed as an indication for aldosterone antagonists. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"How does the diuretic effect of potassium-sparing diuretics compare with loop and thiazide diuretics?","choices":["About the same as thiazides","Stronger than thiazides","Much weaker","Stronger than loops"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Potassium-sparing diuretics have much less natriuretic and diuretic effect than loops or thiazides. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which potassium-sparing diuretic is associated with megaloblastic anemia?","choices":["Triamterene","Amiloride","Spironolactone","Eplerenone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Megaloblastic anemia is the reaction named for triamterene. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A woman starting spironolactone should be told to report which problem?","choices":["Ringing in the ears","Irregular menstrual periods","Sensitivity to sunlight","Yellow-green halos"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Menstrual irregularities are a recognized sex-hormone effect of spironolactone and are worth reporting. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Where on the nephron do carbonic anhydrase inhibitors act?","choices":["Thick ascending limb","Distal convoluted tubule","Proximal tubule","Collecting duct"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors act in the proximal tubule, where bicarbonate is reabsorbed. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Why do amiloride and triamterene reduce urinary potassium loss?","choices":["Potassium reabsorption in the loop rises","They stimulate aldosterone release","The gradient for potassium secretion falls","They block potassium filtration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Less sodium enters the collecting-duct cell, so the gradient that drives potassium secretion into the urine is smaller. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which hormone controls the water channels of the collecting duct?","choices":["Aldosterone","Atrial natriuretic peptide","Renin","Antidiuretic hormone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Antidiuretic hormone acts on its receptor to bring water channel molecules into the luminal membrane. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A patient taking acetazolamide reports unusual sleepiness. Which listed adverse effect does this match?","choices":["Insomnia","Euphoria","Seizures","Drowsiness"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Drowsiness is a listed central nervous system adverse effect of carbonic anhydrase inhibitors. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which laboratory value is most important to monitor in a patient taking an aldosterone antagonist?","choices":["Serum calcium","Serum potassium","Serum uric acid","Blood glucose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hyperkalemia is the main adverse effect, so potassium is the value to follow. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Compared with loops and thiazides, how do aldosterone antagonists affect lipid, glucose and uric acid levels?","choices":["Only modest effects","Marked hyperglycemia","Marked hyperuricemia and gout","Marked hyperlipidemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone antagonists have only modest effects on lipid, glucose and uric acid levels. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How do aldosterone antagonists act at the steroid receptor?","choices":["Block luminal sodium channels","Bind it without reaching the nucleus","Inhibit carbonic anhydrase","Raise aldosterone release"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The antagonist binds the steroid receptor but does not translocate to the nucleus, so the aldosterone message is not delivered. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which combination carries the greatest risk of hyperkalemia?","choices":["Furosemide with hydrochlorothiazide","Spironolactone with lisinopril","Acetazolamide with furosemide","Bumetanide with metolazone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lisinopril is an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor; both drugs raise serum potassium, so together the risk of hyperkalemia is high and potassium must be monitored. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A patient on a loop diuretic has a low potassium. Which drug is added to offset the potassium loss?","choices":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Acetazolamide","Bumetanide","Spironolactone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aldosterone antagonists reduce potassium loss, so they can be combined with a loop diuretic. — Diuretics and Heart Failure Drugs.pptx, Slide 36"}]};
